/*  FREE WATER, NOT BOUND WATER.

    `evaporativeDryer` is the honest counterpart of `solidDryer`: where that
    one models a HYGROSCOPIC material whose moisture is GAB-sorbed and dries
    toward an equilibrium isotherm, this one models a NON-sorbing crystal
    whose moisture is free surface water.  There is no isotherm, so there is
    no equilibrium moisture to stop at -- the drying stops at whichever of
    three real limits binds FIRST, and the unit ANNOUNCES which:

      1. the free water runs out;
      2. the exhaust reaches saturation (the constant-rate air-capacity
         limit);
      3. the gas cannot supply the latent + sensible heat.

    Limits 2 and 3 are coupled -- evaporation cools the gas, which lowers its
    carrying capacity -- and the unit bisects on the removed water until the
    two agree, rather than applying them in sequence and hoping.

    THIS CASE IS THE STARVED ONE.  Identical to evapDryer01_nacl except for
    the gas: 40 kmol/h instead of 600.  The announcement changes to
    [energy-limited]: the solid leaves at the gas's WET-BULB temperature,
    311.68 K, this model's floor, having given up 36.75 kg/h of water with
    the exhaust at a_w 0.886, below its cap, and 16.6 wt% of the water stays
    on the crystal -- a dryer that does not dry, reported as such instead of
    returning a dry solid it cannot produce.

    THE WALL THIS GAS MEETS IS HEAT, AT THE WET BULB -- AND THE CASE'S NAME
    PREDATES THAT (2026-10-05).  The wet solid enters at 298.15 K, BELOW the
    gas's wet bulb, so part of what the gas pays goes to warming the cake up
    to it, and what is left evaporates less than the exhaust has room for.
    The name was given the same morning (DEV.md C35 item 12, it was
    `evapDryer02_energy_limited`), when the floor was still the solid's FEED
    temperature: then the exhaust reached its cap at T_out = 310.73 K, BELOW
    the gas's own wet bulb, and the room wall came first.  Vitor's ruling
    C35 item 7 put the floor at the wet bulb -- a wet solid in a gas cools,
    or warms, toward it -- and that answer is no longer allowed.  Whether
    the case is renamed back, or its wet solid fed warm (at 330 K, above the
    wet bulb, the same gas meets the room wall again: the exhaust at its
    cap at 314.5 K with 49.0 kg/h carried and 51.6 kg/h payable -- the
    fixture `check_evaporative_dryer` arm (a') runs), is Vitor's; DEV.md
    C35 item 7.
*/
units
(
    {
        name        dryer;
        type        evaporativeDryer;
        inputs      ( wetSolid  hotGas );
        outputs     ( drySolid  humidExhaust );
        operation   { }
    }
);
